1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,923
Robinsons and the 1972 Fire:

2
00:00:03,003 --> 00:00:08,150
162 Years of Singapore's
Oldest Department Store

3
00:00:08,150 --> 00:00:13,666
Just before ten o'clock on the
morning of the 21st of November 1972,

4
00:00:13,746 --> 00:00:18,768
an overloaded electrical circuit
caught fire inside Robinsons,

5
00:00:18,848 --> 00:00:21,975
the department store on Raffles Place.

6
00:00:21,975 --> 00:00:25,087
Within minutes the four-storey
building was alight,

7
00:00:25,167 --> 00:00:27,779
and nine people died,
eight of them trapped

8
00:00:27,859 --> 00:00:30,550
in the store's lifts
when the power failed.

9
00:00:30,550 --> 00:00:35,050
Robinsons was 114 years old that morning.

10
00:00:35,050 --> 00:00:38,532
It reopened on Orchard
Road twenty days later,

11
00:00:38,612 --> 00:00:42,714
and it went on trading for
almost half a century more,

12
00:00:42,794 --> 00:00:46,975
until its last Singapore
store closed in January 2021.

13
00:00:46,975 --> 00:00:52,002
The store began on the 1st of
February 1858 as Spicer & Robinson,

14
00:00:52,082 --> 00:00:56,952
a shop at 9 and 10 Commercial
Square opened by Philip Robinson,

15
00:00:57,032 --> 00:01:02,375
who had come to Singapore from
Australia, and James Gaborian Spicer.

16
00:01:02,375 --> 00:01:05,278
Its first advertisement appeared in The

17
00:01:05,358 --> 00:01:08,800
Singapore Free Press on
the 25th of February.

18
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:12,666
The partnership with Spicer
was dissolved the next year,

19
00:01:12,746 --> 00:01:17,750
George Rappa Junior joined, and the
business became Robinson & Company.

20
00:01:17,750 --> 00:01:22,643
The shop moved several times in
its first decades: to the corner

21
00:01:22,723 --> 00:01:27,227
of Coleman Street and North
Bridge Road in 1859, and to new

22
00:01:27,307 --> 00:01:32,125
premises on Battery Road in 1881,
with a tailoring department.

23
00:01:32,125 --> 00:01:35,711
Philip Robinson died in 1886 and his son,

24
00:01:35,791 --> 00:01:39,100
Stamford Raffles Robinson, took over.

25
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:43,231
In about 1891 the store went
back to Commercial Square,

26
00:01:43,311 --> 00:01:47,442
by then renamed Raffles Place,
in much larger premises,

27
00:01:47,522 --> 00:01:51,350
and it stayed on the square
for the next 81 years.

28
00:01:51,350 --> 00:01:55,023
From 1907 the store was
also among the first

29
00:01:55,103 --> 00:01:58,600
agents in the world for Raleigh bicycles.

30
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:01,988
In 1920 the firm became a limited company,

31
00:02:02,068 --> 00:02:07,602
with the businessman Eu Tong Sen
on its board, and in 1928 it opened

32
00:02:07,682 --> 00:02:11,975
its first branch outside
Singapore, in Kuala Lumpur.

33
00:02:11,975 --> 00:02:15,250
The Great Depression hit the store hard.

34
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:19,902
In April 1932 the
directors reported a loss

35
00:02:19,982 --> 00:02:23,863
of $233,745 for the year to January,

36
00:02:23,943 --> 00:02:29,225
and the losses continued
for six years in a row.

37
00:02:29,225 --> 00:02:35,497
Robinsons showed a profit again
only in 1936, a modest $25,355,

38
00:02:35,577 --> 00:02:39,530
and by the year to February 1941 its net

39
00:02:39,610 --> 00:02:44,874
profit had grown to $270,910.
(The Depression's wider

40
00:02:44,954 --> 00:02:50,500
effect on Singapore is the
subject of an earlier post.)

41
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:53,981
In November 1941 the store moved across

42
00:02:54,061 --> 00:02:57,075
the square into Raffles Chambers.

43
00:02:57,075 --> 00:03:01,508
A few weeks later, on the 8th of
December, Japanese bombs damaged

44
00:03:01,588 --> 00:03:04,841
its frontage in the first
air raid on Singapore,

45
00:03:04,921 --> 00:03:09,850
and the next day the store put up a
notice that it was "Open as usual".

46
00:03:09,850 --> 00:03:13,698
It was bombed again on
the 13th of February 1942,

47
00:03:13,778 --> 00:03:17,625
two days before the
surrender, and later looted.

48
00:03:17,625 --> 00:03:22,883
Its chairman, W. H. MacGregor,
died in internment in March 1942,

49
00:03:22,963 --> 00:03:27,800
and the store stayed shut
through the Japanese Occupation.

50
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:32,125
Robinsons reopened in April 1946.

51
00:03:32,125 --> 00:03:37,145
In its first full year,
to the 30th of June 1947,

52
00:03:37,225 --> 00:03:41,724
the company made a net
profit of $1,015,649,

53
00:03:41,804 --> 00:03:46,175
nearly four times its last pre-war figure.

54
00:03:46,175 --> 00:03:51,062
In 1955 the Raffles Place store
became the first department

55
00:03:51,142 --> 00:03:55,440
store in Southeast Asia to
be fully air-conditioned,

56
00:03:55,520 --> 00:04:00,324
and a facelift in 1957 added
the store's name in Malay and

57
00:04:00,404 --> 00:04:04,950
Chinese to its front, as in
the 1971 photograph above.

58
00:04:04,950 --> 00:04:09,846
In 1965 an underground car park
opened beneath Raffles Place,

59
00:04:09,926 --> 00:04:14,575
giving shoppers direct access
to the store's supermarket.

60
00:04:14,575 --> 00:04:20,238
The fire started on the morning of
Tuesday the 21st of November 1972,

61
00:04:20,318 --> 00:04:23,734
with the store full of Christmas stock and

62
00:04:23,814 --> 00:04:28,225
about 350 staff and 200
shoppers and visitors inside.

63
00:04:28,225 --> 00:04:31,362
An overloaded electrical
branch short-circuited,

64
00:04:31,442 --> 00:04:34,513
drapes and displays caught
fire, and the flames

65
00:04:34,593 --> 00:04:37,475
spread through the
building within minutes.

66
00:04:37,475 --> 00:04:40,716
They reportedly rose 200 feet into the air

67
00:04:40,796 --> 00:04:44,275
and could be seen from
Toa Payoh and Jurong.

68
00:04:44,275 --> 00:04:48,375
The power failed, and eight
people were trapped in the lifts.

69
00:04:48,375 --> 00:04:50,910
Six people were rescued from one lift,

70
00:04:50,990 --> 00:04:55,050
but the heat kept firefighters
from reaching the other two.

71
00:04:55,050 --> 00:04:59,782
Nine people died: eight store
employees, one of them a pregnant woman,

72
00:04:59,862 --> 00:05:03,850
and one shopper, who was found
in a second-floor washroom.

73
00:05:03,850 --> 00:05:08,075
The Fire Brigade arrived
at 13 minutes past ten.

74
00:05:08,075 --> 00:05:11,536
Some of the nearby hydrants
had too little pressure,

75
00:05:11,616 --> 00:05:15,225
so the firemen pumped water
from the Singapore River.

76
00:05:15,225 --> 00:05:18,836
Eighteen fire engines were at
the scene by the afternoon,

77
00:05:18,916 --> 00:05:23,175
and the fire was brought under
control at about twenty past twelve,

78
00:05:23,255 --> 00:05:27,075
although the firemen were still
damping it down that night.

79
00:05:27,075 --> 00:05:30,796
The rooftop statue of
Mercury melted, and the roof

80
00:05:30,876 --> 00:05:34,600
of the Overseas Union Bank
next door was damaged.

81
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:38,795
The Stock Exchange's
trading room closed after

82
00:05:38,875 --> 00:05:42,884
85 minutes that morning,
and Robinson shares

83
00:05:42,964 --> 00:05:47,425
fell by as much as $1.20,
to $3.90, on the news.

84
00:05:47,425 --> 00:05:51,978
About 30 cars in the basement
car park came through undamaged,

85
00:05:52,058 --> 00:05:54,225
protected by its thick walls.

86
00:05:54,225 --> 00:05:59,914
Estimates of the loss, covering the
building, the stock and lost trade,

87
00:05:59,994 --> 00:06:03,650
ranged from about $14
million to $21 million.

88
00:06:03,650 --> 00:06:07,802
The Straits Times noted the next
day that the store had stayed

89
00:06:07,882 --> 00:06:12,250
open through two wartime bombings,
only to be destroyed by fire.

90
00:06:12,250 --> 00:06:16,960
On the 21st of December 1972
President Benjamin Sheares

91
00:06:17,040 --> 00:06:22,700
appointed a Commission of Inquiry,
chaired by Justice F. A. Chua.

92
00:06:22,700 --> 00:06:26,043
Its report, released on
the 1st of December 1973,

93
00:06:26,123 --> 00:06:30,724
found that ageing wiring had been
overloaded with festive lighting,

94
00:06:30,804 --> 00:06:34,147
warnings from electricians
had not been acted on,

95
00:06:34,227 --> 00:06:39,038
fire precautions were inadequate,
and large amounts of flammable goods

96
00:06:39,118 --> 00:06:43,100
had been stored in loft areas
that had not been approved.

97
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:47,063
It did not blame the Fire Brigade,
but found that the building

98
00:06:47,143 --> 00:06:50,925
could not have been saved by
the time the firemen arrived.

99
00:06:50,925 --> 00:06:54,396
Many of its recommendations were written

100
00:06:54,476 --> 00:06:57,850
into the Building Control Act of 1974.

101
00:06:57,850 --> 00:07:01,740
Robinsons reopened on the
11th of December 1972 in

102
00:07:01,820 --> 00:07:05,630
the Specialists' Shopping
Centre on Orchard Road,

103
00:07:05,710 --> 00:07:09,600
with new stock flown in
for the Christmas season.

104
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:13,661
The company planned to
rebuild on Raffles Place,

105
00:07:13,741 --> 00:07:18,493
but on the 17th of January
1973 the government announced

106
00:07:18,573 --> 00:07:22,800
that it had acquired the
24,123-square-foot site.

107
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:26,775
My own memory of Robinsons
is of this store.

108
00:07:26,775 --> 00:07:30,412
When I was a boy, my parents
took us to the Specialists'

109
00:07:30,492 --> 00:07:34,275
Shopping Centre to buy things,
I think about once a year.

110
00:07:34,275 --> 00:07:38,928
I also remember hearing about
a big fire, which must have

111
00:07:39,008 --> 00:07:43,825
been the Raffles Place fire
of 1972, the year I turned 12.

112
00:07:43,825 --> 00:07:48,621
Orchard Road was by then becoming
Singapore's main shopping street,

113
00:07:48,701 --> 00:07:53,352
and in 1983 Robinsons moved across
the road into The Centrepoint,

114
00:07:53,432 --> 00:07:56,925
which became its flagship
for the next 30 years.

115
00:07:56,925 --> 00:08:00,972
For a few of those years
it also had a second store

116
00:08:01,052 --> 00:08:06,070
back in the old business district:
a branch in Clifford Centre,

117
00:08:06,150 --> 00:08:09,225
near Raffles Place, from 1977 to 1983.

118
00:08:09,225 --> 00:08:12,852
A branch opened in Raffles City in 2001,

119
00:08:12,932 --> 00:08:18,784
and in 2008 the Dubai-based
Al-Futtaim Group bought the company,

120
00:08:18,864 --> 00:08:22,850
paying $7.20 a share
for 88 per cent of it.

121
00:08:22,850 --> 00:08:25,859
When the Centrepoint lease ended in 2013,

122
00:08:25,939 --> 00:08:30,003
the flagship moved to The
Heeren, also on Orchard Road,

123
00:08:30,083 --> 00:08:33,850
and a suburban store opened
at Jem in Jurong East.

124
00:08:33,850 --> 00:08:37,007
In 2020, the year of
the COVID-19 pandemic,

125
00:08:37,087 --> 00:08:42,126
Robinsons closed its Jem store in
August and The Heeren in December,

126
00:08:42,206 --> 00:08:47,475
and the Singapore company went into
a creditors' voluntary winding-up.

127
00:08:47,475 --> 00:08:51,587
The Raffles City store
sold off its remaining

128
00:08:51,667 --> 00:08:54,662
stock and closed in January 2021,

129
00:08:54,742 --> 00:08:59,400
ending 162 years of Robinsons
stores in Singapore.

130
00:08:59,400 --> 00:09:03,779
In 1980 the Overseas Union
Bank bought the fire site

131
00:09:03,859 --> 00:09:07,981
from the Urban Redevelopment
Authority, and on it

132
00:09:08,061 --> 00:09:12,097
the bank built the OUB
Centre, a 280-metre tower

133
00:09:12,177 --> 00:09:16,550
designed by Kenzo Tange
that was completed in 1986.

134
00:09:16,550 --> 00:09:21,000
It was renamed One Raffles Place in 2008.

135
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,161
Raffles Place itself
is now the heart of the

136
00:09:24,241 --> 00:09:27,850
financial district rather
than a shopping square.

137
00:09:27,850 --> 00:09:31,254
John Little, Robinsons'
old neighbour and rival

138
00:09:31,334 --> 00:09:35,034
on the square, later came
under the same ownership,

139
00:09:35,114 --> 00:09:38,375
and its last Singapore
store closed in 2016.

140
00:09:38,375 --> 00:09:42,815
The Robinsons name was bought
by an Australian company,

141
00:09:42,895 --> 00:09:47,825
Canningvale, and relaunched in
June 2021 as an online store.

142
00:09:47,825 --> 00:09:51,719
The fire's most lasting legacy
is in the building rules,

143
00:09:51,799 --> 00:09:54,983
since the inquiry's
recommendations on wiring,

144
00:09:55,063 --> 00:09:59,250
storage and fire safety went
into the Building Control Act.

145
00:09:59,250 --> 00:10:03,421
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Robinsons was founded

146
00:10:03,501 --> 00:10:07,444
when Singapore was not yet
40 years old, and for most

147
00:10:07,524 --> 00:10:11,775
of its 162 years its story
followed the city's shopping.

148
00:10:11,775 --> 00:10:17,029
It traded on Raffles Place while the
square was the town's commercial centre,

149
00:10:17,109 --> 00:10:20,850
and it moved to Orchard Road
just as the shoppers did.

150
00:10:20,850 --> 00:10:24,378
The fire that ended its time
on the square is remembered

151
00:10:24,458 --> 00:10:27,857
less as the end of a building
than as the beginning of

152
00:10:27,937 --> 00:10:31,915
stricter fire rules, while the
department store itself gave way

153
00:10:31,995 --> 00:10:35,458
to the malls, chains and
online shops that replaced it,

154
00:10:35,538 --> 00:10:39,275
as so many of the world's old
department stores have done.
